infographic compare-binary-horizontal-underline-text-vs
data
  title Two-phase commit vs saga — the deal you accept across services
  desc 2PC keeps all four ACID guarantees but blocks; a saga is ACID minus Isolation but stays available.
  compares
    - label 2PC — one global transaction
      children
        - label Locks held across services
          desc Every participant holds its locks open through the prepare phase, for the length of a network round trip to the slowest service.
        - label Coordinator is a blocking SPOF
          desc If it dies after prepare but before commit, participants are stuck in-doubt — locked, unable to commit or abort — until it recovers.
        - label Full ACID, but unusable
          desc Correct for two tables in one Postgres. Fatal across six services, one of which is a payment gateway you cannot enroll at all.
    - label Saga — local commits plus compensations
      children
        - label No lock held across services
          desc Each step T1..Tn commits locally and immediately; steps are stitched with messages, not locks. You trade isolation for availability.
        - label Undo is hand-written compensation
          desc Every forward Ti ships a compensating Ci — a new business action (a refund, not a delete) that only approximates the undo.
        - label ACID minus the I
          desc Keeps Atomicity, Consistency, Durability; loses Isolation — so dirty reads, lost updates, and non-repeatable reads are now yours to fix.
theme
  palette #f43f5e #14b8a6
